fresh off the presses famed bond  investor bill gross's monthly investment  outlook where he says the reality of  today is that and I quote because this  is perhaps my favorite line in the whole  report not even thin gruel is being  offered to our modern-day Oliver Twist  investors you have to pay to come to the  dinner table and then sit there staring  at an empty plate in other words you're  paying Germany for example to hold your  money right now well what does this mean  for the health of the investing world  what does it mean for the fed what does  it mean for pension plans what does it  mean for you Janice capitals Bill Gross  joins me right now bill welcome back  it's great to have you back on the show  Thank You Trish I guess I anytime you  can work Dickens into an investment  letter along with a few references the  Westminster Dog Show my hat goes off to  you thank you here's another one you  know back in the Depression in the 1930s  humorist Will Rogers said that he wasn't  so much concerned about the return on  his money but the return of his money  and in today's financial markets worked  as you mentioned two trillion dollars of  euroland paper trades at- yield some as  much as minus 75 basis points I think  Will Rogers would have to be concerned  about both a negative yield assures the  investor that not only is the return on  its money or her money unsatisfactory  but the return of her money will be less  than he paid you know it's pretty crazy  and you got investors bill out there  that are getting somewhat desperate  right for yield because you rightly  point out there's there's just none out  there so theoretically let me ask you  could that change should it change do we  anticipate that Yellen will raise rates  in June well I think that's part of the  problem although it's one that can't be  easily diagnosed in terms of historical  models did you get to the extent that  interest rates at the zero bound in the  United States you know promote higher  stock prices higher bond prices you know  some of that trickles down to the real  economy and you know promotes growth but  to be fair not much has you know what  has been promoted has been you know  potential bubbles and  stock markets and in bond markets and I  think the Fed is willing at this point  to at least acknowledge that by raising  interest rates 25 basis points in June i  also think and this is important Trish  that the the interest rate can't be  raised substantially even over the next  two to three years I mean the market is  anticipating a two percent fed funds  rate in 2018 late 2018 and I I think the  reason that they can't is that the US  economy and the global economy to which  the US dollar and the US interest rate  basically serves is the two levered to  highly levered just too much debt and  ultimately two percent is probably that  the top in terms of this Fed cycle let  me ask you though how bill does yellin  and company get off of the zero interest  rate policy at a time when the US dollar  is showing so much strength you talk  about you know the currency war  effectively that we're seeing right now  that nobody wants to call it that but  how can the Fed move when the dollar has  already rallied as much as it has  against other currencies well I think  that's a really good point and I agree  with it obviously I put that in my  investment outlook you know what a  stronger dollar does promote you know  some negatives it promotes not only  lower exports and then lower growth in  terms of manufacturing but it promotes a  an infusion of deflation as opposed to  inflation which is exactly opposite that  what the Fed wants to do today's number  Trish the personal consumption index you  know on a year-on-year basis was at one  point one percent and going lower and  their target is two percent so  ultimately that's again one of the  reasons why you know if they get off the  dime in June or if they get off the dime  in September that they basically have to  go very slowly because ultimately a  stronger dollar promotes lower growth in  the United States as opposed to  haier group you mentioned this lack of  inflation one point one percent versus  what they'd like to see two percent do  we need to be worried about deflation  clearly it's a concern overseas is that  a reality that we could be facing well I  don't think so in the United States I  mean for the most part are our inflation  index is outside of oil and outside of  food prices you know basically reflect  health care expenses and wages in the  united states which are going up by 2%  themselves so the united states that  appears has escaped this you know this  trap this liquidity trap the euro land  in japan are in but you know not  necessarily for all time and so you have  to be worried about it now no but i  think if the Fed truly wants to get to a  two percent inflation rate which by the  way is the reason why they need to get  there is that in a an ensuing recession  or slow down they need some room to  lower rates and so you have to fit  eventually gets to two percent inflation  then that's probably the sweet spot for  Janet Yellen you say eventually two  percent I mean how long is it going to  take for that to unfold do you think  these are going to be very slow moves  bill I do you know I basically think you  have 50 basis points a year right now  the forward market as I mentioned is  anticipating at two percent fed funds  rate in late 2018 so that's three and a  half years whether or not that's the  proper path the proper the slope and in  terms of a forward yield curve no one  really knows but it gets back to you  know what I talked about in april of  last year you know the new neutral rate  the new policy rate which ultimately in  my view has to be much lower than  historically because of high leverage  and because of demographic influences  that basically promote lower structural  growth you know bill Danny Yellen has  ended  that she's going to stay patient but  here we are what six years in deserve  you've had multiple rounds of QE and all  we've got is this anemic growth in your  view has the Fed been too patient I mean  do you worried all that it's policies  have actually had the unintended  consequence of causing people and  companies to delay purchases because  they now money is going to be cheap for  the foreseeable future yeah I think they  went too far Trish and that's a you know  a a subjective assessment of history I  guess but a zero percent interest rate  yeah induces positives in terms of  higher bond prices and stock prices but  also some negatives you know it keeps  zombie corporations alive because they  can borrow it three and four percent as  opposed to eight or nine percent it  destroys business models it's destroying  the pension industry and the insurance  industry because you know basically  their liabilities can't be can't be  provided for by very low interest rates  and ultimately I think it destroys the  you know the capitalistic model at the  margin you talk about stocks and cheap  money you know basically corporations  instead of investing in the real economy  can now simply borrow at you know close  to zero percent and buy their own stocks  which yield two or three percent on a  dividend basis and you know provide a  return of six or seven percent on a  earnings the price ratio basis so you  know there are a lot of negatives in  terms of zero bound interest rates and I  think one of the reasons why the fed  will move is to to sort of gradually  move away from those negatives yeah it's  given birth to all this financial  engineering but you wonder these days  whether investors are really valuing  assets for what they're worth let me ask  you with that in mind you ever worried  that the Fed is just perhaps too  forthcoming in its information  as well I mean they seem to want to tell  us everything and anything that they're  going to do and I just wonder whether  investors have become overly reliant on  that telegraphing from the Fed I mean  the ever long for the days when you  actually had to wait and check the  markets to see what the Fed had done  back before it was as transparent as it  is now well I don't long for that day  because it was it was very uncertain and  you waited on a Thursday afternoon or  morning to see whether or not the Fed  was you know changing its interest rates  on a weekly basis but I think the Fed is  moving away a little bit from this from  this certainty from Draghi's whatever it  takes from you know basically alerting  the market as to you know how high  interest rates are going to go and what  level they should be at so and I think  that's a reflection of Stanley Fischer  he basically wants to move away from the  certainty model will see a little of  that movement but I I'd like to at least  to know a little bit about what the Fed  is thinking in terms of you know new  neutral interest rates where they think  you know the the neutral interest rate  should be relative to the Taylor rule  which they used prior to the recession  to the Great Recession in two thousand  and eight and nine let me let me ask you  this is the first time you and I have  talked on air here since you made your  move to Janice you were founder of pimco  your name was synonymous with that firm  what's it like being someplace new after  having spent your career your entire  career elsewhere well it's certainly  different do you know pimco had 2,500  people Janice Newport you know basically  has seven or eight although Janice  Denver a much larger location and much  larger larger corporation you know has  thousands of people too and and so it's  a little bit different there's more  flexibility basically and what I'm doing  the unconstrained funds that are run on  a separate account basis and the mutual  fund so there's a lot more flexibility  there's the ability to to move as  opposed to  not move so much as was the case in  pimco so you know a little bit smaller  certainly but a little bit more flexible  and I enjoy that you know a lot of  people would have said look I don't need  to do this I got plenty of money I've  had plenty of success why keep going why  I get up and go to the office every day  and do what you do at this point well  part of it you know my wife said she  married me for a better or worse but not  for lunch and that's oh so lunch time  has spent a newport beach but also you  know I wanted to show clients and to  show the world to the extent that  they're interested you know that I can  continue to produce a track record like  I did at PIMCO you know I won't have as  much time I won't have 5 to 10 to 15  years of leeway like I had a pemko in  terms of proving that but certainly for  the next two three or four years you  know I'm a very competitive person and I  like to post numbers that are better  than the market and better than the  competition so it's it's a combination  of both of those well my congrats evil  grows great to see you hope to see you  again soon

